            'The IRS allows the interest deduction only when the money is used to buy, build, or substantially improve the home that secures the loan, not for unrelated expenses like paying off credit cards.',
        ],
        'content' => '<p class="lead">A homeowner with $120,000 of equity and a kitchen remodel to pay for faces a choice most people only make once or twice in a lifetime: borrow a fixed lump sum against the house, open a flexible credit line against it, or refinance the whole mortgage to pull cash out. That choice is what a home equity loan is built to solve, and the mechanics are more specific than most comparison pages let on. In one sentence: most lenders will let you borrow up to 80%-85% of your home\'s value, minus what you still owe on your mortgage, at a fixed rate that averaged around 8.7% in late October 2026 according to Bankrate. The rest of this guide walks through the borrowing limit math, how lenders set your actual rate, what closing costs to expect, the tax-deductibility rule the IRS applies, the real risk of using your home as collateral, and a worked payment example. Run your own numbers with our <a href="{tool:home-loan-calculator}">home loan calculator</a>. If you are weighing this against a full home purchase instead, our <a href="/first-time-home-buyer-guide-2026/">first-time home buyer guide</a> and <a href="/how-much-house-can-i-afford/">how much house can I afford</a> guide cover that side of the math.</p>'
            . '<p><em>Rate and program details change by lender and by week. The figures below reflect national averages reported in late October 2026; check a live rate table, such as Bankrate\'s, before applying. This guide is educational information, not personalized financial or tax advice, and it does not recommend any specific lender.</em></p>'
            . '<h2>How much equity can I borrow against?</h2><p>Lenders calculate your available equity by subtracting your current mortgage balance from a percentage of your home\'s appraised value, usually 80% to 85%, sometimes up to 90% for borrowers with strong credit. This combined figure is called your combined loan-to-value ratio, or CLTV.</p>'
            . '<p>A quick way to estimate it: multiply your home\'s value by 0.85, then subtract your current mortgage balance. On a $400,000 home with a $220,000 mortgage balance, 85% of value is $340,000, minus the $220,000 owed, leaves about $120,000 of maximum borrowing capacity across a loan and any existing second lien. Your actual approved amount also depends on credit score, income, and debt-to-income ratio, so treat this as a ceiling, not a quote.</p>'
            . '<h2>How lenders actually set your rate</h2><p>The national average you see in headlines is just a midpoint; your own quote is built from three inputs lenders weigh most heavily. Credit score tiers matter first: borrowers with scores above roughly 760 generally see the lowest advertised rates, scores in the high 600s to low 700s see a middle tier, and scores below about 680 typically see noticeably higher pricing or tighter approval terms. Combined loan-to-value is the second input: borrowing closer to the 80%-85% ceiling usually costs more than borrowing a smaller share of your equity, because the lender\'s cushion against a price drop is thinner. Debt-to-income ratio, your total monthly debt payments divided by gross monthly income, is the third: most lenders want this below roughly 43%, and a lower ratio can support a better rate alongside strong credit. Loan term also factors in, since shorter terms sometimes price slightly differently than longer ones. None of these inputs work in isolation, which is why two borrowers with the same credit score can still receive different quotes from the same lender. If your score needs work before you apply, see our guide on <a href="/how-to-improve-credit-score-fast-2026/">improving your credit score</a> first, since even a modest score improvement can shift you into a better pricing tier.</p>'
            . '<h2>Home equity loan vs. HELOC vs. cash-out refinance vs. personal loan</h2><p>All but the last of these let you borrow against home equity, and confusing them is the most common mistake homeowners make when comparing options. A personal loan is included here too, since it is the main unsecured alternative worth weighing against the three home-secured products.</p>'
            . '<table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Home equity loan</th><th>HELOC</th><th>Cash-out refinance</th><th>Personal loan</th></tr></thead><tbody>'
            . '<tr><td>How you get the money</td><td>One lump sum at closing</td><td>A revolving credit line you draw from as needed</td><td>Replaces your existing mortgage with a larger one; you keep the difference in cash</td><td>One lump sum, unsecured</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Rate type</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Usually variable</td><td>Fixed or adjustable, set by the new mortgage</td><td>Usually fixed</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Collateral</td><td>Your home</td><td>Your home</td><td>Your home</td><td>None</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Typical rate level (late 2026)</td><td>Roughly 7%-9%, per Bankrate\'s survey</td><td>Similar to home equity loans, often variable</td><td>Tracks current first-mortgage rates</td><td>Often higher than home-secured options, varies widely by credit</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Your existing mortgage</td><td>Stays in place, unchanged</td><td>Stays in place, unchanged</td><td>Replaced entirely, often at a different rate</td><td>Unaffected</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Best for</td><td>A known, one-time expense with a fixed payment</td><td>Ongoing or uncertain expenses, like a phased renovation</td><td>Homeowners who can also improve their primary mortgage rate</td><td>Smaller amounts, or renters and owners without enough equity</td></tr>'
            . '</tbody></table>'
            . '<p>In short: a home equity loan vs. HELOC decision usually comes down to whether you need one lump sum (loan) or flexible access over time (line of credit). A cash-out refinance makes more sense mainly when today\'s mortgage rates are near or below your current rate, since otherwise you are trading a low-rate first mortgage for a higher one. A personal loan avoids putting your home at risk but typically costs more in interest; see our comparison of <a href="/balance-transfer-vs-personal-loan/">balance transfers vs. personal loans</a> if you are weighing an unsecured option instead. For a deeper look at the refinance trade-off, see our guide on <a href="/should-i-refinance-my-mortgage/">whether to refinance your mortgage</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Current home equity loan rates</h2><p>According to Bankrate\'s national lender survey reported October 28, 2026, the average rate stood at 8.69%, with 10-year fixed-rate loans averaging 8.79% and 15-year fixed-rate loans averaging 8.78%. Rates moved earlier in 2026 too: other trackers reported figures in the high-6% and low-7% range at different points in the spring, though different surveys sample different lenders and aren\'t directly comparable to each other. The takeaway is that these rates can shift meaningfully within a single year, so always pull a current quote from a live rate table rather than relying on any single figure, including this one, for more than a few weeks.</p>'
            . '<h2>Closing costs and fees to expect</h2><p>It usually carries smaller closing costs than a first mortgage, but they are rarely zero. Common line items include an appraisal fee to confirm your home\'s current value, an origination fee charged by the lender, a title search to confirm there are no competing claims on the property, and recording fees to register the new lien with your county. Combined, these commonly run in the range of roughly 2% to 5% of the loan amount, though the exact figure varies by lender and some lenders waive specific fees to win your business. On a $50,000 loan, a 3% closing cost estimate works out to about $1,500, money that either comes out of pocket at closing or gets rolled into the loan balance, depending on the lender\'s terms. Ask for an itemized closing disclosure before signing, and compare that total, not just the advertised rate, across lenders.</p>'
            . '<h2>Is home equity loan interest tax deductible?</h2><p>Directly: yes, but only if you use the money to buy, build, or substantially improve the home that secures the loan. IRS Publication 936 states that interest on a home equity loan or line of credit is deductible only when the proceeds are used for that purpose, and this rule applies regardless of when the loan was taken out. If you use a home equity loan to pay off credit cards, cover a child\'s tuition, or take a vacation, that interest is not deductible, even though the loan is secured by your home. If consolidating credit card debt is the actual goal, weigh the tradeoffs in our guide on <a href="/does-debt-consolidation-hurt-your-credit/">whether debt consolidation hurts your credit</a> before using home equity for that purpose specifically.</p>'
            . '<p>For loans taken out after December 15, 2017, the deductible mortgage interest (combining your first mortgage and any qualifying home equity debt) is capped at a $750,000 principal balance, or $375,000 if married filing separately. Most homeowners also need to itemize deductions, rather than take the standard deduction, to benefit at all. Check current IRS Publication 936 and a tax professional for your specific situation; this guide is not personalized tax advice.</p>'
            . '<h2>What happens if you fall behind on payments</h2><p>This is secured debt, meaning your home itself backs the loan, the same general structure as your first mortgage. If payments are missed for an extended period, the lender can generally pursue foreclosure to recover what is owed, just as a mortgage lender could on the primary loan. In practice, lenders typically contact a borrower well before that point, and options such as a repayment plan, a loan modification, or refinancing the balance can sometimes prevent it. The practical lesson is not to treat the fixed monthly payment as optional: because the collateral is your home, the consequence of sustained non-payment is materially different from an unsecured debt like a credit card, where the main risk is to your credit score rather than your house.</p>'
            . '<h2>Pros and cons</h2><ul><li><strong>Pro: a fixed rate and payment.</strong> Unlike most HELOCs, your rate and monthly payment do not change over the loan term.</li><li><strong>Pro: often a lower rate than unsecured debt.</strong> Because the loan is secured by your home, rates are typically well below credit card APRs.</li><li><strong>Pro: potential tax deduction.</strong> If the funds go toward home improvement, the interest may be deductible.</li><li><strong>Con: your home is collateral.</strong> Missing payments puts your home at risk of foreclosure, unlike most unsecured debt.</li><li><strong>Con: closing costs.</strong> Many lenders charge appraisal, origination, or title fees similar to a first mortgage, though some waive them.</li><li><strong>Con: less flexible than a HELOC.</strong> You receive the full amount upfront and start paying interest on all of it immediately, even if you do not need it all right away.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Worked example: monthly payment and total cost</h2><p>Assume a homeowner borrows $50,000 with a 15-year fixed home equity loan at 8.78%, close to Bankrate\'s late-October 2026 average for that term.</p>'
            . '<table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody>'
            . '<tr><td>Loan amount</td><td>$50,000</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Rate (fixed)</td><td>8.78%</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Term</td><td>15 years (180 months)</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Estimated monthly payment</td><td>about $501</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Total paid over the full term</td><td>about $90,120</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Total interest paid</td><td>about $40,120</td></tr>'
            . '</tbody></table>'
            . '<p>If this homeowner instead chose a 10-year term at Bankrate\'s reported 8.79% average for that term, the payment rises to roughly $628 a month, but total interest drops to about $25,330, a savings of nearly $14,800 in exchange for a higher monthly payment. Run your own loan amount, rate, and term with our <a href="{tool:home-loan-calculator}">home loan calculator</a>. For a side-by-side look at how points and fees change the real cost of borrowing against a home, see our guide on <a href="/mortgage-points-explained/">mortgage points explained</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>How to apply for a home equity loan</h2><ol><li><strong>Check your estimated equity</strong> using your home\'s current value and your mortgage payoff balance.</li><li><strong>Check your credit score and debt-to-income ratio,</strong> since both affect your approved rate and amount.</li><li><strong>Get quotes from at least three lenders,</strong> including your current mortgage servicer, a local credit union, and an online lender, since rates and fees vary meaningfully.</li><li><strong>Compare the APR, not just the rate,</strong> since closing costs and fees change the true cost.</li><li><strong>Confirm the tax treatment</strong> of your intended use of funds before assuming any interest deduction.</li></ol>'
            . '<h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2><ul><li><strong>Assuming all home equity interest is deductible.</strong> It is only deductible when the funds buy, build, or substantially improve the home securing the loan.</li><li><strong>Borrowing the maximum CLTV just because you qualify.</strong> A smaller loan amount means a smaller fixed payment and less risk if your home\'s value dips.</li><li><strong>Comparing only the interest rate.</strong> Closing costs, appraisal fees, and any prepayment penalty change the real cost; compare APR.</li><li><strong>Confusing it with a HELOC.</strong> A fixed lump sum and a variable revolving line solve different problems.</li><li><strong>Not shopping multiple lenders.</strong> Rates on these products vary more between lenders than first-mortgage rates typically do.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/mortgage-points-explained/">Mortgage points explained</a></li><li><a href="/should-i-refinance-my-mortgage/">Should I refinance my mortgage?</a></li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Sources</h2><p>IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p936" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Publication 936, Home Mortgage Interest Deduction</a>; Bankrate <a href="https://www.bankrate.com/home-equity/current-interest-rates" rel="noopener" target="_blank">current home equity loan and HELOC rates</a> (reported October 28, 2026); Bankrate <a href="https://www.bankrate.com/home-equity/how-big-can-home-equity-loan-be/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">how big can a home equity loan be</a>; Experian <a href="https://www.experian.com/blogs/ask-experian/how-much-can-i-borrow-with-heloc/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">how much you can borrow with a HELOC</a>. Rate figures move frequently; check a live rate table before applying.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational information and not personalized financial or tax advice. It does not endorse or recommend any specific lender.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs' => [
            [ 'How much equity can I borrow against with a home equity loan?', 'Most lenders cap combined borrowing at 80%-85% of your home\'s current value, minus what you still owe on your mortgage, though some lenders allow up to 90% for well-qualified borrowers. A rough estimate: multiply your home\'s value by 0.85 and subtract your mortgage balance.' ],
            [ 'What is the difference between a home equity loan and a HELOC?', 'A home equity loan pays out as a single lump sum at a fixed rate with a fixed monthly payment. A HELOC is a revolving credit line, usually at a variable rate, that you can draw from, repay, and draw from again during a set draw period.' ],
            [ 'Is home equity loan interest tax deductible?', 'Only if the loan proceeds are used to buy, build, or substantially improve the home that secures the loan, according to IRS Publication 936. Interest on one used for other purposes, such as paying off credit card debt, is not deductible, and most homeowners need to itemize to benefit at all.' ],
            [ 'What are current home equity loan rates?', 'Bankrate\'s national lender survey reported an average home equity loan rate of 8.69% as of October 28, 2026, with 10-year loans at 8.79% and 15-year loans at 8.78%. Rates move regularly, so check a current rate table before applying.' ],
            [ 'Is a home equity loan or a cash-out refinance better?', 'A home equity loan leaves your existing mortgage untouched and adds a separate fixed-rate loan, which usually makes sense if your current mortgage rate is good. A cash-out refinance replaces your entire mortgage, which can make sense if today\'s rates are close to or below your existing rate.' ],
            [ 'How is the monthly payment on a home equity loan calculated?', 'It is calculated like any fixed-rate installment loan, using the loan amount, the interest rate, and the term in months. For example, a $50,000 loan at 8.78% over 15 years runs about $501 a month. Use a home loan calculator to check your own numbers.' ],
            [ 'What closing costs come with a home equity loan?', 'Typical charges include an appraisal fee, an origination fee, a title search, and recording fees, commonly totaling roughly 2% to 5% of the loan amount depending on the lender. Some lenders waive certain fees, so ask for an itemized closing disclosure before comparing offers.' ],
            [ 'What happens if I can\'t pay my home equity loan?', 'Because the loan is secured by your home, sustained missed payments can lead to foreclosure, the same general risk that applies to a first mortgage. Contacting your lender early about a repayment plan or modification is generally better than letting payments lapse.' ],
            [ 'What credit score do I need for a home equity loan?', 'Requirements vary by lender, but most want a credit score in the mid-600s or higher, with the best rates reserved for scores above about 760. Your debt-to-income ratio and combined loan-to-value also affect approval and pricing.' ],
        ],
    ],
    [
        'slug'       => 'when-are-taxes-due',
        'title'      => 'When Are Taxes Due? 2026 and 2027 Federal Tax Deadlines',
        'keyword'    => 'when are taxes due',
        'desc'       => 'When are taxes due in 2027? The federal filing deadline, extension rules, quarterly estimated tax dates, business deadlines, and late-filing penalties.',
        'excerpt'    => 'The federal tax filing deadline for 2027, how extensions work, quarterly estimated tax due dates, business entity deadlines, and what happens if you miss the deadline.',
        'answer'     => 'The federal deadline to file and pay 2026 individual income taxes is Thursday, April 15, 2027. Filing Form 4868 by that date grants an automatic extension to file until October 15, 2027, but it does not extend the deadline to pay any tax owed, which is still due April 15.',
        'publish_at' => '2026-11-04 21:00',
        'takeaways'  => [
            'The federal individual tax deadline for the 2026 tax year is Thursday, April 15, 2027; it shifts only when April 15 falls on a weekend or legal holiday, which it does not in 2027.',
            'Form 4868 extends your time to file until October 15, 2027, but not your time to pay; unpaid tax still accrues penalties and interest from April 15.',
            'S corporations and partnerships generally file a month earlier than individuals, around March 15, while military members in a combat zone and residents of federally declared disaster areas can get separate, specific extensions.',
            'Self-employed people and others without withholding generally owe quarterly estimated taxes, due April 15, June 15, and September 15 of the tax year, and January 15 of the following year.',
        ],
        'content' => '<p class="lead">Every spring the same question floods search bars: <strong>when are taxes due</strong>? Thursday, April 15, 2027, is the answer for the 2026 tax year, worth stating plainly because so much advice online still carries older dates or hedges about a weekend shift that does not apply this cycle. April 15, 2027 is simply a Thursday, and it is not a federal holiday, so nothing pushes the date. What trips people up is everything around that one date: the difference between a filing extension and a payment extension, separate deadlines for business entities and the self-employed, special rules for military and disaster situations, and the penalty math if a date slips by. That is what the rest of this guide untangles, with links to our <a href="/year-end-tax-planning-checklist/">year-end tax planning checklist</a> and the <a href="{tool:income-tax-calculator}">income tax calculator</a> where they are useful.</p>'
            . '<p><em>Dates and penalty rates below reflect IRS rules as published. Verify any date on IRS.gov before treating it as final. This is educational information, not personalized tax or legal advice.</em></p>'
            . '<h2>So, when exactly are taxes due in 2027?</h2><p>The federal deadline to both file and pay your 2026 tax year return is Thursday, April 15, 2027. The IRS\'s standard rule only moves that date to the next business day when April 15 lands on a Saturday, Sunday, or a legal holiday, including the Emancipation Day holiday observed in Washington, D.C. Neither applies in 2027, which is why this particular tax season has one clean date rather than the "the 15th, or the following Monday" caveat attached to some other years.</p>'
            . '<h2>An extension buys you time to file, not time to pay</h2><p>Here is the part people get backwards most often: Form 4868, filed by April 15, 2027, grants an automatic six-month extension, pushing your paperwork deadline to October 15, 2027. No approval is needed; filing the form is enough. But the extension only covers the filing of your return. Whatever you owe is still due April 15, 2027, and the IRS begins charging late-payment penalties and interest from that date whether or not you have a valid extension on file.</p>'
            . '<p>The practical fix is simple: estimate your balance and pay something close to it by April 15, then finish the actual paperwork later under the extension. A reasonable payment on time avoids most of the penalty exposure described further down, even with the return itself still pending. April 15 is also the standard deadline for making a prior-year IRA contribution, so if you still want to fund a traditional or Roth IRA for the 2026 tax year, that window closes the same day; check your contribution room with the <a href="{tool:ira-calculator}">IRA calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Different deadlines for different business entities</h2><p>Not every return follows the April 15 individual date. S corporations (Form 1120-S) and partnerships (Form 1065) generally file a month earlier, due the 15th day of the third month after the entity\'s tax year ends, which for a calendar-year business commonly lands in mid-March. An extension filed on Form 7004 adds roughly six more months. C corporations (Form 1120) generally follow the individual April 15 date for a calendar-year entity, with an extension to October 15. Owners of pass-through entities should check both their business return deadline and their personal April 15 deadline, since the two can require separate action.</p>'
            . '<h2>Military, combat zone, and disaster-area extensions</h2><p>Two groups of taxpayers get deadline relief that is specific to their situation rather than a blanket calendar shift. Service members serving in a designated combat zone, and others serving in direct support of the armed forces there, generally receive an extension of at least 180 days after their last day in the combat zone (or after a combat-zone-related hospitalization ends), plus however many days were left before the normal deadline when their service began. According to the IRS\'s own combat zone guidance, this extension covers both filing and payment, not filing alone.</p>'
            . '<p>Separately, the IRS regularly postpones deadlines for taxpayers in specific counties or states after a federally declared disaster, such as a hurricane, wildfire, or severe storm system. These relief announcements are issued locality by locality throughout the year, and the postponed date varies by event, so a single fixed date cannot be stated here reliably. If you live in or have a business in an area that experienced a recent disaster, check the IRS\'s "Tax relief in disaster situations" page on IRS.gov for the specific counties covered and the exact postponed date that applies to you.</p>'
            . '<h2>Deadlines for amended returns</h2><p>If you need to correct a return you already filed, Form 1040-X is the standard route, and it carries its own timing rule separate from the original filing deadline. Under the general statute of limitations, you generally have until the later of three years from when you filed the original return, or two years from when you paid the tax, to file an amended return and still claim a refund. This window is about claiming money back; it is a different concept from the April 15 filing or payment deadlines above. Specific circumstances, such as a disaster postponement or a bad-debt deduction, can change this window, so confirm your own situation with the IRS or a tax professional.</p>'
            . '<h2>Does every state follow the same calendar?</h2><p>Most states with an income tax set their own filing deadline to match April 15, so the federal date usually works as a mental shortcut. It is not universal, though: a handful of states run on a different date, or layer on their own holiday shifts and disaster-relief extensions independent of what the IRS does. Confirm directly with your state\'s department of revenue rather than assuming the federal date carries over automatically, especially if your state or county has an active disaster declaration. However the deadline itself shakes out, the bigger lever is usually how much taxable income you are reporting in the first place; our <a href="/retirement-planning-how-much-need-2026/">retirement planning guide</a> and <a href="{tool:401k-calculator}">401(k) calculator</a> cover how pre-tax contributions earlier in the year reduce what you owe by April.</p>'
            . '<h2>If you\'re self-employed, April 15 is only one of four dates</h2><p>Freelancers, contractors, and anyone else without tax withheld from a paycheck generally owe estimated tax four times a year, not once. The IRS threshold is simple: if you expect to owe $1,000 or more for the year after subtracting withholding and credits, quarterly payments are generally expected.</p>'
            . '<table><thead><tr><th>Payment</th><th>Covers income earned</th><th>Due date</th></tr></thead><tbody>'
            . '<tr><td>Q1</td><td>January 1 - March 31, 2027</td><td>April 15, 2027</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Q2</td><td>April 1 - May 31, 2027</td><td>June 15, 2027</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Q3</td><td>June 1 - August 31, 2027</td><td>September 15, 2027</td></tr>'
            . '<tr><td>Q4</td><td>September 1 - December 31, 2027</td><td>January 18, 2028 (January 15 is a Saturday, and the following Monday is the MLK Day holiday)</td></tr>'
            . '</tbody></table>'
            . '<p>Missing a quarterly payment can trigger an underpayment penalty even if the annual return is filed and paid in full by the following April. The quarterly clock and the annual clock are tracked separately. For ways to manage what you owe before the year closes, see our guide to <a href="/tax-loss-harvesting/">tax-loss harvesting</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>What actually happens if the deadline slips past you</h2><p>Two separate penalties, plus interest, can stack up, and they compound faster than most people expect:</p>'
            . '<ul><li><strong>Failure-to-file penalty:</strong> generally 5% of the unpaid tax for each month or part of a month the return is late, capped at 25%. This is the heavier of the two, which is exactly why filing or extending matters even when you cannot pay.</li><li><strong>Failure-to-pay penalty:</strong> generally 0.5% per month, also capped at 25%. When both penalties overlap in the same month, the failure-to-file rate is reduced by the failure-to-pay amount, so the combined rate usually lands near 5% a month rather than 5.5%.</li><li><strong>Interest:</strong> accrues on unpaid tax and on unpaid penalties, at the federal short-term rate plus 3%, reset quarterly and compounded daily.</li></ul>'
            . '<p>Put in dollars, here is what that looks like: a filer who owes $6,000 and neither files nor pays until July 20, roughly four months late with no extension on file, ends up owing about $1,200 in combined failure-to-file and failure-to-pay penalties (the two run together at a combined 5% of the unpaid balance per month, up to the cap) plus roughly $160 of interest at an illustrative 8% annual rate, for a total closer to $7,360 than the original $6,000. Filing Form 4868 and paying even a partial amount by April 15 would have eliminated the much larger failure-to-file portion of that bill, leaving only the smaller failure-to-pay penalty and interest on whatever balance remained. Run your own numbers with the <a href="{tool:income-tax-calculator}">income tax calculator</a> before deadline day arrives.</p>'
            . '<h2>A short checklist for the weeks before April 15</h2><ol><li><strong>Estimate your liability early,</strong> even from incomplete records.</li><li><strong>File Form 4868 if your paperwork won\'t be ready,</strong> securing the extension to October 15.</li><li><strong>Pay what you can toward the estimate by April 15</strong> regardless of whether the return itself is filed yet.</li><li><strong>Mark all four quarterly dates on your calendar</strong> if you\'re self-employed, not just the annual one.</li><li><strong>Check whether a disaster postponement applies to your county</strong> on IRS.gov before assuming the standard date.</li><li><strong>Apply for an IRS payment plan</strong> instead of simply not paying if the full balance isn\'t realistic.</li></ol>'
            . '<h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/year-end-tax-planning-checklist/">Year-end tax planning checklist</a></li><li><a href="/tax-loss-harvesting/">Tax-loss harvesting explained</a></li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Sources</h2><p>IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/node/145181" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS marks 70th anniversary of April 15 tax filing deadline</a>; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-4868" rel="noopener" target="_blank">About Form 4868</a>; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/payments/failure-to-file-penalty" rel="noopener" target="_blank">failure-to-file penalty</a> and <a href="https://www.irs.gov/payments/failure-to-pay-penalty" rel="noopener" target="_blank">failure-to-pay penalty</a> pages; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-1040-es" rel="noopener" target="_blank">About Form 1040-ES</a>, estimated tax for individuals; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/extension-of-deadlines-combat-zone-service" rel="noopener" target="_blank">extension of deadlines, combat zone service</a>; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/tax-relief-in-disaster-situations" rel="noopener" target="_blank">tax relief in disaster situations</a>; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-1040-x" rel="noopener" target="_blank">About Form 1040-X</a>. Quarterly dates for the 2027 estimated-tax cycle and business-entity deadlines are drawn partly from secondary tax-calendar sources and should be confirmed against the IRS\'s own published calendar closer to each due date.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational information and not personalized tax or legal advice.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs' => [
            [ 'When are taxes due in 2027?', 'The federal deadline to file and pay your 2026 tax year individual income tax return is Thursday, April 15, 2027. The date does not shift in 2027 because April 15 falls on a weekday that is not a federal holiday.' ],
            [ 'What is the tax deadline if I file an extension?', 'Filing Form 4868 by April 15, 2027, extends your filing deadline to October 15, 2027. It does not extend your payment deadline; any tax owed is still due April 15, 2027, regardless of the extension.' ],
            [ 'When do S corporations and partnerships have to file?', 'Form 1120-S for S corporations and Form 1065 for partnerships are generally due the 15th day of the third month after the entity\'s tax year ends, commonly around mid-March for a calendar-year business, with a six-month extension available through Form 7004.' ],
            [ 'What happens if I miss the tax deadline?', 'You can face a failure-to-file penalty of generally 5% of unpaid tax per month, up to 25%, a failure-to-pay penalty of generally 0.5% per month, up to 25%, and interest on the unpaid balance. Filing on time or filing an extension, even if you cannot pay in full, avoids the much larger failure-to-file penalty.' ],
            [ 'What are the quarterly estimated tax due dates?', 'For income earned in 2027, the standard due dates are April 15, June 15, and September 15, 2027, and January 18, 2028 for the fourth installment, shifted from January 15 because that date is a Saturday followed by the MLK Day holiday. These apply mainly to the self-employed and others without tax withholding.' ],
            [ 'Do military members get a different tax deadline?', 'Service members in a designated combat zone, and others serving in direct support there, generally receive an extension of at least 180 days after their service ends, plus any days that remained before the normal deadline when their service began, covering both filing and payment according to IRS guidance.' ],
            [ 'How long do I have to file an amended return for a refund?', 'Generally, the later of three years from when you filed the original return or two years from when you paid the tax, under the general statute of limitations for Form 1040-X refund claims. Specific situations can change this window, so confirm with the IRS or a tax professional.' ],
            [ 'Do state tax deadlines match the federal deadline?', 'Most states with an income tax use the same date as the federal deadline, but a few states set their own date or apply separate extensions, including disaster-specific postponements. Check your state\'s department of revenue website to confirm.' ],
            [ 'Who has to pay quarterly estimated taxes?', 'Generally, anyone who expects to owe $1,000 or more in tax for the year after subtracting withholding and credits, which commonly includes self-employed workers, freelancers, and people with significant investment or rental income without tax withheld.' ],
            [ 'Can I get a payment plan if I can\'t pay my taxes by the deadline?', 'Yes. The IRS offers short-term and long-term payment plans, which generally reduce the ongoing failure-to-pay penalty rate compared with not paying at all. Apply through IRS.gov rather than simply missing the deadline.' ],
        ],
    ],
    ];
}
